# Sovereign cloud (uksh sov-dev / sov-prod). Layered over default.env.
#
# Sized for the stated population of ~100 clinicians, NOT measured under load -
# revisit once the API and database tiers have run with real traffic. Rationale
# and cost impact are in the DiMeAs sovereign cost model.

# api CPU/memory are deliberately NOT overridden here - they inherit default.env
# (10m/256Mi, which Autopilot raises to its ~250m/512Mi pod floor).
#
# Raising them looks like an obvious improvement and is actually a regression
# risk today. Zero-downtime deploys currently work because ReadWriteOnce means
# one NODE, not one pod: the surge pod is small enough that Autopilot bin-packs
# it onto the node already holding the api-images disk, both pods mount it, and
# the old one retires only once the new one is Ready. At 1 vCPU / 2 GiB a second
# pod no longer fits beside the first, Autopilot provisions a new node, and the
# surge pod cannot attach the disk - turning a working rollout into a stalled one.
#
# So this bump belongs in the same change as moving api-images to ReadWriteMany,
# not before it. The throttling it would fix is also unmeasured: at ~100 users
# roughly 20 concurrent SSE streams are I/O-bound, and 0.25 vCPU may well be
# adequate. Measure event-loop lag before spending the risk.
# Two replicas, now that api-files-rwx.yaml gives the api pod ReadWriteMany
# volumes - ReadWriteOnce attaches to one node and was the actual ceiling.
# Requires api_files_rwx = true in terraform for this environment.
# EINE api-Replica. Nicht aus Kapazitaetsgruenden - api ist bei 100 Nutzern zu
# rund 3 Prozent ausgelastet - sondern weil LibreChat mit mehreren Replicas
# fehlerhaft streamt.
#
# Beobachtet am 2026-08-28 in dev UND prod: Die Antwort wird erzeugt und in
# MongoDB gespeichert, erscheint aber erst nach einem Reload. Ursache ist
# prozesslokaler Stream-Zustand - jeder Pod meldet beim Start
# "[StreamServices] Created in-memory stream services". Wird die Generierung auf
# Pod A gestartet und der Stream von Pod B gelesen, kennt B den Job nicht.
#
# Sticky Sessions reichen NICHT. GENERATED_COOKIE mit 3600s ist auf dem
# BackendConfig UND dem GCP-Backend-Service aktiv - der Fehler trat trotzdem in
# beiden Umgebungen auf. Es ist ein Rennen, also intermittierend.
#
# MAX ebenfalls 1: Eine HPA, die unter Last auf 2 skaliert, wuerde den Fehler
# genau dann ausloesen, wenn das System beschaeftigt ist.
#
# Voraussetzung fuer >1: gemeinsamer Zustand ueber Redis (USE_REDIS=true).
# Die Org-Policy sperrt Memorystore, nicht Redis selbst - ein Redis im Cluster
# ist moeglich, aber echte Arbeit und kein Schritt vor dem Pilotstart.
API_MIN_REPLICAS=1
API_MAX_REPLICAS=1

# Sized up from the default.env baseline of 10m / 256Mi, which assumed an api
# tier of several replicas behind an HPA. At the single replica pinned above,
# that assumption is gone: this one pod IS the api tier, so there is neither
# horizontal headroom nor failover, and an OOMKill is a full application
# outage rather than a degraded one.
#
# 10m is a REQUEST, not an allocation. The container sets no limits, so on GKE
# 1.35 Autopilot it has been surviving by bursting into idle node capacity -
# best-effort, and throttled back to 1% of a core under node contention.
# Memory has no equivalent escape hatch: exceed the request and it is killed.
#
# Sized for 300-400 pilot users. That load is only ~2-3 concurrent requests and
# is not itself demanding - the point is real headroom above idle on a tier that
# cannot currently fail over. Costs roughly EUR 40-60/month per environment at
# Autopilot list rates.
#
# Revisit once Redis lands and API_MAX_REPLICAS can go above 1.
API_CPU=1000m
API_MEMORY=2Gi
# Rollout strategy is deliberately NOT set here - it differs between dev and
# prod, so it lives in sov-dev.env. See the trade-off note in default.env.

# WiredTiger sizes its cache from available memory; 512Mi gave it almost none.
# Disk grown because conversation history is never pruned by the application.
MONGO_CPU=1
MONGO_MEMORY=4Gi
MONGO_DISK=100Gi

# 100Mi could not hold what this database exists to store: an empty PostgreSQL
# data directory is already ~40MB, and a bge-m3 vector is 1024 dimensions (~4KB)
# before chunk text and the index.
VECTORDB_CPU=1
VECTORDB_MEMORY=4Gi
VECTORDB_DISK=100Gi

# Meilisearch memory-maps its index and is latency-sensitive. Single replica by
# necessity - the open-source build has no clustering.
MEILI_CPU=500m
MEILI_MEMORY=2Gi
MEILI_DISK=20Gi

# CPU-bound while chunking documents and driving embedding calls to TEI.
# --- api document uploads (LibreChat paths.uploads) ---------------------------
# ~100 clinicians doing document analysis: a few thousand PDFs at a few MB each.
# 10Gi would be tight and expansion is one-way, so start with room.
UPLOADS_DISK=50Gi

# Two replicas: rag is stateless (no PVC, only a secret), so a second one is
# free redundancy for RAG retrieval. Single-replica rag was a hard-down
# dependency for document search.
RAG_REPLICAS=2
RAG_CPU=1
RAG_MEMORY=2Gi

# --- Moderation & rate limiting ----------------------------------------------
# Measured against sov-dev on 31.08.2026. The stock values assume LibreChat is
# reached directly; behind the GCE ingress they behave very differently.
#
# WHAT WAS MEASURED: express resolves req.ip to the LAST X-Forwarded-For entry,
# not the client. Proven by exhausting the login bucket (LOGIN_MAX=20) for one
# (client, proxy) pair: a DIFFERENT client behind the SAME proxy hop was already
# limited, while the SAME client behind a DIFFERENT proxy hop was not. Behind a
# Google load balancer, which appends "<client-ip>, <lb-ip>", that last entry is
# the load balancer - one value shared by every user.
#
# WHY THAT MATTERS: both the message rate limiter and the ban are keyed on
# req.ip. banViolation writes the ban to the user AND to req.ip, and checkBan
# blocks on either. So one user crossing BAN_INTERVAL bans the shared load
# balancer address and locks out EVERY user for BAN_DURATION (2 h).
#
#   NON_BROWSER_VIOLATION_SCORE - must stay BELOW BAN_INTERVAL (20). At the
#     stock 20 a single non-browser request trips the ban instantly, with no
#     accumulation needed. This was hit by accident during load testing.
#   LIMIT_MESSAGE_USER - the per-USER limiter keys on req.user.id and is immune
#     to how the proxy chain resolves. This is the protection that should be
#     load bearing, not the IP one.
#   MESSAGE_IP_MAX - while req.ip stays shared this is a GLOBAL cap. 40/min
#     across all users does not survive a morning login burst at 300-400 users,
#     so it is raised to a coarse backstop now that the user limiter is on.
#   TRUST_PROXY - 2 makes req.ip the real client behind the load balancer
#     (socket=front-end, +1=lb, +2=client). Spoof-safe, because the balancer
#     appends the true client address to the right of anything a client sends.
#     VERIFY against a real request through the ingress; nothing above depends
#     on it being exactly right, which is why it is not the only change.
NON_BROWSER_VIOLATION_SCORE=5
LIMIT_MESSAGE_USER=true
MESSAGE_IP_MAX=2000
TRUST_PROXY=2
